No matter how smart the mind is, if it doesn't have the tools to put what is on its mind into action, its productivity and use cases decline dramatically.
It would be a golfer who has amazing course management skills, great golf swing, but not having the golf clubs nor a golf ball. 
Same thing for AI. LLMs are amazing. These models, trained on so much data through optimized methods, are very advanced and better than humans at most things.
They are becoming more and more capable.
But Agentic AI takes it to a whole new level.
It gives these models 'tools'. It allows these models to effectively take action by themselves, rather than telling humans to take actions in certain ways.
Efficiency goes up, productivity goes up, less need for human intervention, and most of all, there are more use cases. Makes things easier.

Is that a good thing, though?
These AI models are actually taking meaningful actions now, so the slight hints of error and hallucinations could be emphasized. Possibly a snowball effect.
With agentic AI, the growth of reasoning mechanisms, DFIR, and cyber defending/investigations and understanding AI behaviors couldn't be more essential.
